<article class="env-lemma" id="07GV">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07GV">Lemma <span data-tag="07GV">23.3.2</span>.</a> The category of divided power rings has all limits and they agree with limits in the category of rings. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The empty limit is the zero ring (that's weird but we need it). The product of a collection of divided power rings $(A_ t, I_ t, \gamma _ t)$, $t \in T$ is given by $(\prod A_ t, \prod I_ t, \gamma )$ where $\gamma _ n((x_ t)) = (\gamma _{t, n}(x_ t))$. The equalizer of $\alpha , \beta : (A, I, \gamma ) \to (B, J, \delta )$ is just $C = \{ a \in A \mid \alpha (a) = \beta (a)\} $ with ideal $C \cap I$ and induced divided powers. It follows that all limits exist, see Categories, Lemma <a href="/tag/002N" data-tag="002N">4.14.11</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>